<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · c. 1513 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Pastime with Good Company</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi>Pastime with Good Company</bdi> / <bdi>The Kynges Balade</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song written by King Henry VIII of England in praise of hunting, singing, dancing and the company of friends.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Henry VIII</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Henry VIII</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the English royal court under Henry VIII</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>the royal court, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early 16th century; oldest copy in the Henry VIII Manuscript, c. 1513</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Henry VIII; oldest copy c. 1513)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastime-with-good-company">Pastime with Good Company</a>&quot;, also known as &quot;The King's Ballad&quot; (&quot;The Kynges Balade&quot;), is an English song written by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> in the early 16th century. It is regarded as the most famous of the king's creative works, and it became a popular song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and other European countries during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Like every man of noble birth in his age, Henry was expected to master fencing, hunting, dancing, writing poetry, singing, and playing and composing music, and he was educated in all of these as a prince; his contemporaries thought him a talented composer and poet.</p><p>The oldest known version is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a> of about 1513, now in the British Library, a collection of works of his authorship signed &quot;By the King's Hand&quot;, which also holds two masses, a motet, an anthem and other songs and ballads, for voices and for instruments. It is thought that the song may have been written for Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>. It was presumably played at court with the king's other compositions, but its simple and catchy melody made it a popular tune beyond the court, soon heard at fairs, in taverns and at other gatherings. It is believed to have been one of the favourite pieces of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. Contemporary documents and publications refer to it, and musicians made many variants and instrumental arrangements of it in the following years; The Complaynt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, written in 1548, lists &quot;Passetyme with gude companye&quot; among the songs popular in Scotland in the early part of the century.</p><p>The song belongs to the first years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s reign, a time of exuberance and extravagance at the English court, made possible by the kingdom's political stability and the wealth of the royal finances. Banquets and feasts were held continually, as were outdoor sports and pastimes: hunting, hawking, jousting and archery tournaments. The young king was himself a skilled sportsman, excelling at riding, archery, wrestling and real tennis. The song praises these entertainments and the carefree mood of the court, and gives them a moral defence: good company is better than idleness, for idleness breeds vice.</p><p>It remains a favourite of choirs and has been recorded with lute, recorder, trombone, percussion and flute, among other instruments; its early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> melody is often heard in films and documentaries about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and the Tudors. Rock and folk groups have taken it up too: Jethro Tull as &quot;King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s Madrigal&quot;, Gryphon on their self-titled album of 1973, Blackmore's Night as &quot;Past Time with Good Company&quot;, and the Swingle Singers in 1989.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A young king's defence of pleasure. The singer means to love good company, to hunt, sing and dance, as long as he lives, and will let no one stop him. Its later verses give the reason: company keeps a man from idleness, from which every vice comes, and so the best course is to choose good company and shun the bad. The mood is brisk, confident and cheerful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Pastime with good company</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I love and shall until I die</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>grudge who lust but none deny</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>so God be pleased thus live will I</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>for my pastance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>hunt sing and dance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>my heart is set</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>all goodly sport</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>for my comfort</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>who shall me let</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Youth must have some dalliance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>of good or ill some pastance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Company methinks then best</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>all thoughts and fancies to digest.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>for idleness</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>is chief mistress</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>of vices all</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>then who can say.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>but mirth and play</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>is best of all.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Company with honesty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>is virtue vices to flee.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Company is good and ill</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>but every man has his free will.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>the best ensue</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>the worst eschew</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>my mind shall be.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>virtue to use</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>vice to refuse</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>thus shall I use me.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Henry VIII, British Library Add MS 31922 (c. 1513), in modern spelling as printed on English Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastime_with_Good_Company" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
